# In dulci jubilo, BWV 751 (attributed to J. S. Bach; by Johann Michael
# Bach)
#
# Attribution: BWV 751 was catalogued under J. S. Bach in the 19th
# century, but the Neumeister manuscript gives it to Johann Michael
# Bach (1648-1694), J. S. Bach's father-in-law; Mutopia itself marks it
# "spurious". The title should credit J. M. Bach.
#
# Character: a cradle-song setting of the Christmas chorale for organ:
# the melody in long notes in the soprano, a quietly rocking alto in
# quarters, and a drone in the pedal, like a shepherd's bagpipe. Where
# the chorale sings "Alpha es et O" the pedal leaves its drone and takes
# up the melody in canon with the soprano. Gentle and luminous, never
# heavy.
#
# Time signature: 3/2, 73 bars in the Mutopia score (Edition Peters) and
# in the MIDI. Bar 1 is a full bar of rests with the soprano's G as its
# last half note, so the bar numbers match the score with no pickup.
# Three MIDI tracks, but the alto changes staff in the source, so many
# of its notes sit in the soprano's track: track order cannot be used
# for part priority, and no more than three parts sound anyway.
# The pedal's E2, F-sharp2, G2 and A2 lie below B2 and are lifted an
# octave by the converter. The G drone sounds an octave higher than
# written, still below the manuals, but the pedal canon's B2, C3 and D3
# are not lifted, so its line jumps down an octave in the middle of
# each "Alpha es et O" (listen for it in bars 30 and 66).
#
# Tempo: half = 132 (dotted whole = 44, one bar), 219 halves, about
# 1:42. The one confirmed recording timing: Franz Haselboeck (organ,
# "Collected Organ Chorales", Apple Music) 1:37, about half = 135. I
# could not find timings for other organists. 132 is close to
# Haselboeck: a rocking one-in-a-bar, slow enough for the long melody
# notes, quick enough that they do not fade away on the chip.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the phrase names are the chorale's; the plan is my own reading):
#   1-17   G major over a G pedal (G held for 25 bars): "In dulci
#          jubilo" and "Nun singet und seid froh" (1-9), "Unsers
#          Herzens Wonne" and "Leit in praesepio" (9-17), soprano.
#   18-25  The manuals in rocking quarters, the hands interlocking (the
#          phrases "Und leuchtet als die Sonne / Matris in gremio"
#          dissolved into figuration), still over the G pedal.
#   26-37  "Alpha es et O": soprano 26-32, the pedal in canon two bars
#          behind (28-34); close on D (37).
#   38-53  The chorale again, now in D major over a D pedal (held
#          37-61).
#   54-61  The rocking quarter figuration again, in D.
#   62-73  "Alpha es et O" in G: soprano 62-68, pedal canon 64-69, then
#          the G pedal returns under the close.

tempo 132 2          # half note; three to the 3/2 bar

voice top 0          # the chorale melody leads throughout
voice mid 1          # the rocking alto a step back
voice bottom 1       # the drone a step back too; the pedal canon is
                     # brought forward with lead below

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-17: the chorale's first phrases over the
                     # G drone
dyn 18 mp            # bars 18-25: the interlocking figuration, a softer
                     # manual
dyn 26 f             # bars 26-37: "Alpha es et O", the pedal canon:
                     # fuller registration
lead 28-34 bottom    # the pedal's canon on the melody
broaden 36-37 0.92   # hold back into the close on D

dyn 38 mf            # bars 38-53: the chorale in D major
dyn 54 mp            # bars 54-61: the figuration again, softer
dyn 62 f             # bars 62-73: "Alpha es et O" in G, to the end
lead 64-69 bottom    # the pedal canon

rit 3 0.7            # bars 71-73: the last phrase and the held G
